VERDICT: REJECTED

The claimed end-to-end pipeline is not runnable from its documented/default training path. `scripts/train.py` creates `SyntheticWeightDataset` samples shaped as 4-channel weight images, then passes them as classification images to the host model, which requires 3 channels. It also selects a 128KB payload (1,048,576 bits) while the pipeline’s default encoder accepts only 1,024 bits; the same mismatch exists in the default `PipelineConfig` and TOML values. The CLI does not construct encoder/decoder configs from the payload size, and its nested TOML structure is read as flat keys, so its advertised config overrides do not apply.

Additionally, the only pipeline/experiment tests use the custom `tiny` backbone, while CLI choices exclude it and production ResNet/MobileNet/VGG paths reconstruct BatchNorm running-stat buffers through differentiable tensors. `functional_call`/BatchNorm rejects gradients with respect to running statistics, making normal training on the advertised backbones fail. The 151 tests therefore do not validate the supported execution path.

Security: `scripts/evaluate.py` and checkpoint loading use default `torch.load`, which can execute code when supplied an untrusted checkpoint. Restrict checkpoints to trusted sources or use `weights_only=True` with an explicitly supported checkpoint format.

Next actions:
1. Make payload dimensions consistent end-to-end and parse/map nested TOML sections into all pipeline/experiment settings.
2. Replace the synthetic training input with real 3-channel image data (or a deliberately separate synthetic image dataset); do not feed weight representations to the host classifier.
3. Fix parameter reconstruction to leave BatchNorm buffers non-differentiable/current, then add a minimal actual ResNet/MobileNet training-step integration test.

Memory note: the added memory entries include code-specific paths, sizes, and step-by-step test/debug recipes; retain only durable principles rather than code-derivable implementation details.

